  "title": "How to Set Up and Solve Proportion Equations with Powers",
  "content": "<p>At Higher tier, proportion can involve powers and roots:</p><p><strong>Direct proportion to a power:</strong> \\(y \\propto x^n\\) means \\(y = kx^n\\). Common cases: \\(y \\propto x^2\\), \\(y \\propto x^3\\), \\(y \\propto \\sqrt{x}\\).</p><p><strong>Inverse proportion to a power:</strong> \\(y \\propto \\frac{1}{x^n}\\) means \\(y = \\frac{k}{x^n}\\).</p><p>The key steps are always: (1) write the proportion statement, (2) replace ∝ with = k, (3) find k, (4) write the equation, (5) use it.</p><p>Common mistake: forgetting to square/cube the x-value before multiplying by k. If \\(y = 3x^2\\) and \\(x = 4\\), then \\(y = 3(16) = 48\\), not \\(3(4) = 12\\).</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>\\(y\\) is directly proportional to \\(x^2\\). When \\(x = 3\\), \\(y = 45\\). Find \\(y\\) when \\(x = 4\\).</strong></p><p>\\(y = kx^2 \\Rightarrow 45 = k(9) \\Rightarrow k = 5\\)</p><p>\\(y = 5(4^2) = 5(16) = 80\\)</p>"
 },
